    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The sudden failure of the refrigeration unit is a crisis, and you must act swiftly to prevent the tuna from becoming a meal for the unwanted guests - the bacteria. You need to take immediate action to minimize loss and ensure food safety. \n\nFirst, isolate the affected area of the truck to prevent cross-contamination. This is crucial, as the bacteria can spread quickly, and you don't want your precious tuna to become a snack for the local wildlife. You must act fast, like a team of skilled, ninja-like refrigeration specialists, to contain the situation.\n\nNext, assess the situation and determine the extent of the damage. Check the temperature logs to see how long the tuna has been exposed to the warm temperatures. This will help you determine the severity of the situation and the potential for bacterial growth. \n\nNow, regarding food safety protocols, you need to check the following: \n\n1. The truck's refrigeration unit was properly maintained and serviced before the trip.\n2. The tuna was stored at the correct temperature (below 40°F) during the entire trip.\n3. The truck's refrigeration unit was functioning correctly before the failure.\n4. The tuna was properly packaged and sealed to prevent leakage or contamination.\n5. The crew followed",
